"""Original digital-abuse awareness; no device forensics or unsafe real exercises."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-055","Technology Facilitated Abuse and Digital Safety","technology-facilitated-abuse-and-digital-safety","Technology Facilitated\nAbuse and Digital Safety",
 "Develop a fictional survivor-centred digital-support outline that recognises technology-related coercion, preserves choice and privacy and routes technical and legal questions to qualified roles.",[
m("Digital abuse power and evidence","Recognise technology-related control while distinguishing reports, research findings and technical verification.",[
"Technology can be used to threaten, monitor, impersonate, exclude or distribute material without consent. Such behaviour can connect online spaces with offline control over relationships, income, movement or reputation. Ask about the behaviour and its effects on choice and access rather than assume online harm is less serious or entirely separate from a person's circumstances. Conversely, a troubling device symptom does not establish who caused it or whether surveillance occurred. An introductory learner should distinguish what a person reports from what has been technically verified, without requiring forensic proof before respectful support is offered. [S17, S1]",
"Gender, disability, language, age, sexuality and access to independent devices can affect how a person experiences harm and seeks assistance. Technology may also be essential for work, support networks or accessible communication. Telling someone simply to leave the internet can remove valuable resources and shift responsibility away from the person using abuse. A survivor-centred response asks what matters to the person and what forms of contact or assistance are feasible. It avoids treating a platform feature, device ownership or a demographic category as a complete explanation of the problem. [S17, S1]",
"The 2026 regional scoping review maps studies of technology-facilitated GBV in sub-Saharan Africa. It reports uneven geographic coverage and substantial attention to university and young populations. A map of reported forms and experiences does not estimate representative regional prevalence or prove that the same tactic affects everyone in the same way. The measurement scoping review also identifies gaps in what existing instruments capture. Neither article supplies a diagnostic test for a person's phone or validates a universal Nigerian checklist for deciding whether a reported experience is real. [R27, R28]",
"Generative AI can amplify impersonation and non-consensual fabricated content, but a learner should not authenticate an image, request intimate material or produce an abusive demonstration. Respond to the reported concern within the appropriate support role and route specialist technical or legal questions through approved arrangements. Course exercises contain descriptions only; do not upload passwords, private messages, identifying screenshots or sexual images. In particular, never solicit or circulate sexual material involving children. A non-graphic fictional account is enough to assess careful reasoning without creating further exposure or attempting a criminal investigation. [S17, S1]"],
"Fictional case: an adult reports threats involving a fabricated image and says her shared device is also needed for work. A learner recognises the access and coercion concerns without requesting the image, diagnosing spyware or instructing her to abandon the device.",
"Write a support-analysis note separating the reported behaviour, access consequence, technical uncertainty, person-led priority and appropriate qualified role. Name material that must not be requested for this coursework.",
"A useful note takes the concern seriously while preserving evidence status. It connects digital and offline circumstances, avoids blame and diagnostic shortcuts, and uses no sensitive images or credentials.",["S17","S1","R27","R28"],[
("Digital and offline control may interact.","A fictional person reports online threats affecting work and movement. What is a relevant support perspective?","Consider the connected effects on choices, access and offline circumstances.",["Treat online conduct as harmless by definition.","Assume digital and offline experiences cannot overlap.","Require forensic proof before listening respectfully."],"Technology-related behaviour can have consequences across settings."),
("A device symptom is not a spyware diagnosis.","A fictional phone loses battery quickly and a learner declares surveillance confirmed. What is the accurate correction?","The symptom alone does not establish surveillance or identify an actor.",["Battery loss conclusively identifies a perpetrator.","All unusual device behaviour proves the same software is present.","A classroom inference is equivalent to a forensic examination."],"Technical attribution requires appropriate expertise and evidence."),
("Digital access can be a resource as well as a concern.","An adult depends on a shared device for income and support. What should shape assistance?","Her priorities, access needs and specialist safety planning rather than a blanket disconnection instruction.",["Assume loss of income is irrelevant.","Insist everyone permanently leaves online services.","Blame the person for needing digital access."],"Removing technology may also remove essential resources."),
("A regional scoping review does not estimate representative prevalence.","A planner treats the SSA scoping review as the rate of abuse in every Nigerian community. What is missing?","Representative sampling and context-specific evidence; the review maps unevenly covered studies.",["Only a more colourful chart.","Proof that all populations were equally represented.","An assumption that every study had identical measures."],"A scoping map cannot supply an unmeasured local prevalence rate."),
("Course assessment does not require sensitive digital material.","A learner offers a private intimate screenshot as evidence for the digital-safety worksheet. What fits the exercise?","Use the supplied non-graphic fictional description and do not submit the screenshot.",["Upload it to a shared course folder.","Ask classmates to authenticate the image.","Collect passwords to investigate the account."],"Learning should not create further exposure or become a forensic investigation."),
]),
m("Safe contact and specialist planning","Prepare a person-led support outline before suggesting technical or reporting changes.",[
"A communication route that is convenient for the worker may be unsafe or inaccessible for the person seeking support. Before an actual contact, authorised staff need to agree appropriate channel, timing, message content and what to do if contact is interrupted. Do not assume an email address is private, that a shared phone is independently controlled or that a missed message means consent to another method. In the fictional exercise, identify these planning questions without sending test messages, contacting a real account or placing sensitive information in a calendar or class submission. [S18, S17, S1]",
"Changing access can alter what another person notices. Removing a device, changing credentials, deleting content or making a platform report may affect safety, evidence or continued access to work and support. There is no universal action sequence suitable for every situation. The NNEDV guide is a specialist planning comparator with 2018 US context, not a current Nigerian technical or legal protocol. Use its planning principle to explain why qualified advice and the person's priorities should precede a proposed change; do not reproduce a device checklist or present old symptom examples as reliable surveillance tests. [S18, S1]",
"An introductory worker can acknowledge the concern, clarify role and service limits and facilitate appropriate specialist options through approved arrangements. The worker should not demand passwords, log in to an account, install a monitoring app, bypass access controls or direct a confrontation. A qualified technology-abuse specialist may be needed alongside GBV, legal or clinical support, depending on the person's needs. Verify the actual service's scope and safe contact conditions rather than promise that a platform, police unit or advocate will remove content, accept a case or achieve a particular outcome. [S17, S18]",
"A bounded support outline records only necessary planning information within authorised systems and explains confidentiality limits appropriately. It should identify who can take the next step, what remains undecided and how the person can revisit preferences. Avoid promising that private browsing, a VPN or any single setting guarantees safety. Technology changes and local processes require current specialist knowledge. A course completion badge demonstrates completion under the academy's approved assessment requirements; it does not grant authority for forensic work, independent safeguarding decisions or unsupervised technical intervention on someone else's device. [S18, S17, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a support worker plans to email an adult about a digital-abuse referral, but the adult has not agreed that email is private. The learner pauses the proposed contact and identifies safe-contact and specialist-planning questions without testing the account.",
"Prepare a conditional next-step note with preferred contact questions, two possible consequences of unplanned account changes, role limits, a qualified referral dependency and a way to revisit preferences.",
"A sound outline avoids assuming channel safety and does not prescribe blanket resets or reporting. It connects person-led priorities with current specialist advice and makes the next action conditional on appropriate arrangements.",["S18","S17","S1"],[
("Safe contact needs agreement rather than worker convenience.","A fictional adult has not agreed that email is private. What should precede a sensitive referral message?","Appropriate agreement on channel, timing and content through authorised arrangements.",["Send the full history because email is fast.","Assume an address proves sole account control.","Try several contact methods until someone answers."],"Convenient communication is not automatically safe communication."),
("Technical changes can have safety and access consequences.","A learner proposes immediately deleting an account after a reported threat. What is the appropriate planning correction?","Consider the person's priorities and possible safety, evidence and access effects with qualified advice.",["Deletion always has no consequences.","An account reset is universally safe.","The learner's preference replaces the person's needs."],"Unplanned changes may alter risks or remove resources and information."),
("Older specialist guidance needs explicit context limits.","The NNEDV page carries a 2018 copyright and US examples. How should it be used here?","As a bounded planning comparator with current local specialist advice still needed.",["As a 2026 Nigerian reporting law.","As a guaranteed diagnosis from a single device symptom.","As proof that every listed service operates locally."],"Date and jurisdiction limits should remain visible."),
("Support does not require access to credentials.","A learner offers to collect a person's password to investigate a fictional concern. Which boundary applies?","Do not collect credentials or access accounts; identify appropriate specialist support.",["Store the password in the worksheet for convenience.","Share the account with classmates to check it.","Install monitoring software as a course exercise."],"Introductory support is not account access or technical investigation."),
("No single privacy setting guarantees safety.","A fictional plan says private browsing guarantees that an abusive actor cannot learn about help-seeking. What correction is needed?","Remove the guarantee and consider the actual context with qualified safety planning.",["Keep the claim because one feature solves all risks.","Assume every device is independently controlled.","Replace discussion of priorities with a universal app recommendation."],"A feature-specific assertion cannot establish overall safety."),
]),
m("Reporting information and accountable follow-up","Distinguish survivor choice, technical evidence handling and programme claims without exposing material.",[
"Platform reporting, legal processes and specialist evidence handling are different activities. A report button does not guarantee removal, confidentiality, protection or preservation of information. Requirements vary by service, platform, jurisdiction and circumstance. A learner should explain the need for qualified current advice without promising an outcome or giving a universal instruction to save or delete material. Do not ask a person to forward intimate images, access an abusive actor's account or gather evidence through confrontation. Actual urgent safeguarding concerns should follow authorised procedures, not a classroom decision tree. [S17, S18, S1]",
"Consent to one support purpose does not authorise copying a person's account into a presentation, a training database or a public advocacy story. Removing names may not protect identity if a screenshot, location, role or combination of details remains recognisable. Course submissions use only fictional descriptions, with no personal records. An authorised service should use its approved information-handling procedures and explain relevant limits honestly. The worker's desire to demonstrate helpfulness, document an impressive case or obtain a platform response does not override purpose limitation, confidentiality or the person's informed choices. [S1, S17]",
"Measures of technology-facilitated sexual violence vary in what they include and whose experiences they reflect. The 2026 measurement scoping review catalogues measures but does not appraise study quality or establish their robustness. Its coverage is heavily Western high-income, and its focus is narrower than all GBV. Counting reports, removed posts or accounts changed cannot by itself demonstrate that harm ended or that a support programme caused improvement. A local evaluation would need a relevant construct, suitable methods, ethical arrangements and appropriate validation rather than a copied questionnaire selected because it appears in a review. [R28, R27]",
"Follow-up should be feasible, agreed and limited to the appropriate role. A fictional note can identify the person-led goal, the dependency on a qualified service, the agreed contact question and a point to revisit unresolved needs. A completed referral offer differs from assistance accessed, and removal of one item differs from sustained safety or recovery. Do not blame the person if a platform action fails or preferences change. Keep uncertainty and service constraints visible, seek supervision when a concern exceeds scope and review whether the process itself introduced burdens or unwanted exposure. [S18, S17, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a project counts platform reports as successful recovery and wants to use a private account story in a promotional slide. A learner separates the output from the unsupported outcome and rejects reusing the account without an appropriate purpose and permissions.",
"Write a follow-up and reporting critique: output observed, outcome not established, privacy concern, specialist dependency and agreed review question. Use no platform account, image or real incident data.",
"A careful critique distinguishes platform actions from safety outcomes, uses the measurement evidence within its limits and keeps support information out of unapproved secondary uses.",["S17","S18","S1","R27","R28"],[
("A platform report does not guarantee a protection outcome.","A fictional support note promises that using a report button will remove all risk. What is defensible instead?","Explain uncertain outcomes and seek appropriate current specialist advice within the person's priorities.",["Treat submission of a report as guaranteed recovery.","Promise that every platform preserves evidence identically.","Assume reporting cannot affect safety or access."],"Platform actions and personal safety are distinct and context-dependent."),
("Support-purpose consent does not authorise publicity.","A service wants to turn a private digital-abuse account into course promotion. What remains necessary?","Appropriate separate purpose, permissions and risk review; the support purpose alone is insufficient.",["Permission inferred from accepting support.","Automatic reuse if names are removed.","A claim that publicity is always in the person's interests."],"Information gathered for support is not automatically available for secondary use."),
("A measure catalogue does not establish validity.","A planner selects a questionnaire solely because the JMIR scoping review lists it. What is missing?","Appraisal of construct, validation, context, permissions and ethical suitability.",["Proof that every listed tool is Nigerian-validated.","A conclusion that scoping reviews certify all instruments.","Permission to copy every questionnaire item."],"The review maps measures without quality appraisal or universal validation."),
("Content removal is different from sustained safety.","One reported post is removed. Which follow-up claim is appropriately limited?","The item was removed; wider needs and agreed goals require separate review.",["All offline and online harm necessarily ended.","The platform action proves clinical recovery.","No further person-led review can be relevant."],"A specific action does not establish every broader outcome."),
("Changing preferences should remain possible.","A fictional adult no longer wants the initially discussed platform action. What should a support outline allow?","Revisiting preferences and appropriate options through agreed safe contact and qualified support.",["Continuing solely because the worker already wrote the plan.","Treating a changed preference as failure by the person.","Publishing the account to pressure agreement."],"Person-led support recognises changing choices and circumstances."),
])])
